  "hints": [
    "Checking every pair is O(n^2). For a fixed j, which value must the earlier element have?",
    "Sweep left to right keeping a table: value -> how many times it has appeared so far. For a[j] you need the count of (k - a[j]).",
    "Add cnt[k - a[j]] to the answer BEFORE inserting a[j]; this counts each pair exactly once and handles the case a[j] = k - a[j] correctly."
  ],
  "editorial": "Sweep `j` from left to right while keeping `cnt[v]`, the number of earlier positions holding value `v`. The number of valid partners for position `j` is exactly `cnt[k - a[j]]`; add it to the answer and *then* increment `cnt[a[j]]`. Doing the lookup before the insertion counts each unordered pair once and never pairs an element with itself.\n\nWith a hash map every lookup/insert is expected `O(1)`, so the total is expected `O(n)`. In C++ prefer a randomised hash (or a sort-based approach) for `unordered_map` on contest sites, where adversaries craft colliding keys. The count can reach `n(n-1)/2`, which overflows 32-bit integers for `n ≳ 65 000`.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
